course: dictionary
lesson: 10646
title: Unimpressed
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unimpressed means not thinking that someone or something is good, special or clever. It can also mean you are a little annoyed or not pleased, often with a flat look. Context helps you choose the right meaning. «Lily just looked unimpressed, and Mr Hughes, right behind him, heard every word.»
explain: Lily did not smile or cheer. Her flat face shows she was not won over, and she was a bit annoyed. Authors use this word to show a character's reaction, and it is often funny.
explain: Look for the phrases you may meet: «look unimpressed», «be unimpressed by» or «with» something, and «distinctly unimpressed», which means very clearly not pleased.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in "not pleased" or "not thinking it's good" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Unimpressed means not thinking that someone or something is good, special or clever.
fact: Unimpressed can also mean a little annoyed or not pleased, often with a flat look.
fact: Useful phrases are «look unimpressed», «be unimpressed by» and «be unimpressed with».
fact: «Distinctly unimpressed» means clearly and definitely not pleased.
fact: Writers often use unimpressed for a cat, a teacher or a parent who is not won over.
exercise: «Lily just looked unimpressed, and Mr Hughes, right behind him, heard every word.» What does unimpressed mean here? | *Not pleased, with a flat look | Very excited and jumping about | Lost and looking for the way home
exercise: Which word means not thinking that something is good, special or clever? | Cheerful | Generous | Thrilled | *Unimpressed
exercise: The cat yawned and turned its back on the fancy new bowl. What does this show? | The cat was hungry for dinner | The cat loved the bowl | The cat was lost | *The cat was unimpressed by the bowl
test: Mum looked unimpressed when Sam showed her his muddy shoes. Which word could replace unimpressed? | Thrilled | Delighted | *Not pleased | Amazed
test: The judges were unimpressed by the tricks, so they gave no points. What does this tell you? | The judges thought the tricks were brilliant | The judges wanted to try the tricks | *The judges did not think the tricks were good | The judges were scared of the tricks
exam: Which sentence uses unimpressed correctly? | *The cat looked unimpressed by the fancy new bath toy | The baby was unimpressed and laughed at the bubbles | The crowd cheered and threw their hats in the air, completely unimpressed | The dog was unimpressed, so it jumped up and wagged its tail with joy
